{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1315637744/14/codegen_prompts/1.md\"","aggregated_output":"你要为一个新的 ARC-AGI 风格题目实现代码。代码文件必须包含 `main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray` 和 `generate_input() -> np.ndarray`。\n\n## 新题位置\n\n- 描述目录：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1315637744/14`\n- 题号：`1`\n- 目标代码路径：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1315637744/14/1.py`\n\n## 新题描述\n\n<new_puzzle index=\"1\">\n- `description`：根据封闭区域内部的孔洞数量，从颜色图例条中选取对应颜色，对该区域进行重新填充。\n\n- `Input structure`：\n  - 背景为黑色（black）。\n  - 网格中存在若干由同一种非背景颜色（如 grey）绘制的封闭不规则形状，每个形状的边界宽度为 1 像素，内部可能包含 0 到若干个黑色孔洞（即被边界完全包围但未被填充的黑色连通区域）。\n  - 网格的最底部一行或最右侧一列存在一个颜色图例条：图例条由若干连续的单色段组成，每段长度为 1 像素，从左到右（或从上到下）依次排列不同的颜色，分别对应孔洞数 0、1、2、3……例如第一个颜色对应 0 个孔洞，第二个颜色对应 1 个孔洞，以此类推。\n\n- `Rule`：\n  - 保留所有封闭形状的边界像素不变。\n  - 对每个封闭形状，数出其内部包含多少个独立的黑色孔洞（4-连通区域）。\n  - 用孔洞数作为索引，在颜色图例条中查找对应颜色（索引从 0 开始）。\n  - 将该封闭形状内部所有非孔洞的黑色像素填充为查找到的颜色；孔洞保持黑色。\n  - 输出中移除颜色图例条（将其恢复为背景色）。\n\n- `Key insight`：封闭形状本身的颜色和大小无关紧要，决定填充颜色的是每个形状内部孔洞的数量，而图例条提供了从数量到颜色的映射关系。解题者需要先识别图例条的位置和含义，再对每个形状计数孔洞并完成颜色匹配。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n## 机制相似参考题\n\n下面参考题由本地相似度流程自动选择：先用 `annotations`/docstring/代码特征抽取机制标签，再按机制相似度、文本相似度和代码特征相似度重排，并用 MMR 做去重。优先学习这些题的对象识别方式、随机生成器约束、边界条件处理，不要照搬旧题规则。\n\n已选参考：eval/dbff022c, eval/62593bfd, train/278e5215, eval/8b7bacbf, eval/71e489b6, eval/16b78196, train/902510d5, eval/e3721c99\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"dbff022c\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3931\nmechanism=0.4988\nlexical=0.0123\ncode_feature=0.4286\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, object:closed_region, object:grid_edge, object:hole, object:sprite, object:template, operation:flood_fill, relation:direction, relation:inside, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/dbff022c.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/dbff022c.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nFill hollow sprites' interiors using a color-palette legend on the grid edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Several hollow sprites with distinct border colors.\n- A small 2×N or N×2 palette block sits on one grid edge; one row/column lists border colors, the other lists corresponding fill colors.\n\nRule:\n- Locate the palette legend on a grid edge and read it as a border→fill color mapping.\n- Find enclosed bg regions (holes) inside sprites that don't touch the grid boundary.\n- For each hole, identify the most common adjacent border color, look it up in the palette, and fill the hole with the mapped fill color.\n\nKey insight: The palette's edge position and orientation determine reading direction; border colors in the palette uniquely key into fill colors.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"summary\": \"Use a color palette legend on the grid edge to fill sprites' interiors based on their border color mapping.\",\n  \"type\": \"transform_in_place\",\n  \"src\": \"hollow sprites with colored borders s\",\n  \"dst\": \"interior of same sprite d\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"d.fill_color == palette_lookup(s.border_color)\",\n  \"path\": \"\",\n  \"trajectory\": \"\",\n  \"transform\": \"fill d with palette[s.border_color]\",\n  \"symbol_instructions\": \"palette is 2xN or Nx2 block on grid edge; read direction based on edge position: top edge reads top-to-bottom, bottom edge reads bottom-to-top, left edge reads left-to-right; first row/column is border color, second is fill color\",\n  \"multi_step\": \"Step 1: locate 2xN or Nx2 palette legend on grid edge; Step 2: build color mapping (border -> fill) based on reading direction; Step 3: for each hollow sprite, lookup border color in palette and fill interior with corresponding fill color\",\n  \"concepts\": [\n    \"color-mapping\",\n    \"lookup-table\",\n    \"flood-fill\",\n    \"border-detection\"\n  ],\n  \"notes\": \"The challenge is correctly reading the palette legend based on its edge position and orientation\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"62593bfd\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.2983\nmechanism=0.3340\nlexical=0.0130\ncode_feature=0.5714\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:counter, code:rotation, object:grid_edge, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:shape_match, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/62593bfd.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/62593bfd.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nSort objects to top or bottom edge by whether their shape (up to rotation/flip) is shared.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Several multicolor connected components (8-connected) scattered across the grid.\n\nRule:\n- Compute each object's canonical shape (binary mask, invariant to rotation and flip).\n- Objects whose shape appears in two or more components → translate to TOP edge (row 0).\n- Objects whose shape is unique → translate to BOTTOM edge (row H−h).\n- Horizontal position (left column) is preserved; only vertical translation changes.\n\nKey insight: The sorting criterion is shape frequency (rotation-invariant), not area — duplicated shapes go up, unique shapes go down.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_in_order\",\n  \"src\": \"all connected components (objects) on the background\",\n  \"dst\": \"translate each object d vertically to an edge (x preserved)\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"group objects by their pixel-count (area). If an area value occurs >=2 times, those objects go to the TOP edge; if the area is unique, that object goes to the BOTTOM edge\",\n  \"transform\": \"duplicated-size -> top; singleton-size -> bottom\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"278e5215\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.2984\nmechanism=0.3333\nlexical=0.0160\ncode_feature=0.5714\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, code:counter, object:hole, object:sprite, object:template, operation:count, relation:inside, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/278e5215.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/278e5215.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nRecolor the large mask crop column-by-column using the separate three-row palette strip.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the most common color.\n- One large monochrome foreground object is the mask; its bbox is the output region and may contain background holes.\n- The remaining foreground is a 3-row palette rectangle with the same width as the mask bbox: two identical color rows and one uniform row.\n\nRule:\n- Crop to the mask object's bbox.\n- For each output column, color mask pixels with the corresponding color from the repeated palette rows.\n- Replace background holes inside the crop with the palette's uniform third-row color.\n\nKey insight:\nThe grey object is only a stencil; its columns select colors from the legend, while its holes expose the legend's fill color.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"palette column s in the separate 3-row strip\",\n  \"dst\": \"column d in the mask bbox crop\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"s.column == d.column_in_crop\",\n  \"transform\": \"recolor mask pixels in d with s.repeated_row_color; fill background holes with the palette's uniform third-row color\",\n  \"symbol_instructions\": \"3-row palette strip maps each mask-crop column to its mask color; the uniform third row gives the hole color\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"crop to mask bbox\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"8b7bacbf\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3096\nmechanism=0.3507\nlexical=0.0129\ncode_feature=0.5714\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, code:counter, object:closed_region, object:grid_edge, object:hole, operation:count, operation:flood_fill, relation:inside\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/8b7bacbf.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/8b7bacbf.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nFill enclosed regions along a line seeded by unique border pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Colored lines/drawings on the grid.\n- Unique single-pixel colors on the grid border mark seed endpoints of each line.\n\nRule:\n- Each border seed pixel connects (8-connected) to a line drawing.\n- Find closed bg cavities whose boundary touches that line.\n- Flood-fill each such cavity with the seed pixel's color.\n\nKey insight: Only cavities reachable through the seed's own line get filled; the seed's unique color acts as a per-line fill label.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"summary\": \"Find single pixels on grid edges that are endpoints of lines, trace along the line to find connected closed regions, and fill those regions with the edge pixel's color.\",\n  \"type\": \"move_along_path\",\n  \"src\": \"single colored pixels s on grid edge that are endpoints of a line\",\n  \"dst\": \"closed regions d connected to the line\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"d is reachable by following the line from s\",\n  \"path\": \"trace along the continuous line from edge pixel s\",\n  \"trajectory\": \"fill interior of each closed region d encountered along path with s.color\",\n  \"transform\": \"d.interior.color = s.color\",\n  \"symbol_instructions\": null,\n  \"multi_step\": \"Step 1: identify single pixels on grid edge; Step 2: trace the line connected to each edge pixel; Step 3: for each closed region touching the line, flood-fill its interior with edge pixel color\",\n  \"concepts\": [\n    \"flood-fill\",\n    \"path-tracing\",\n    \"edge-detection\",\n    \"region-filling\"\n  ],\n  \"notes\": \"\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"71e489b6\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.2908\nmechanism=0.3724\nlexical=0.0163\ncode_feature=0.2857\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, object:grid_edge, object:hole, object:sprite, operation:count, relation:inside\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/71e489b6.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/71e489b6.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nHighlight black defects in blue foreground by surrounding them with orange.\n\nInput structure:\n- Blue (1) foreground on black (0) bg; small black holes or protrusions within blue regions; occasional thin blue spurs into black areas.\n\nRule:\n- Thin blue pixels mostly surrounded by black are absorbed into the background.\n- Small black components (≤3 cells) and leaf pixels of larger black components are \"defects.\"\n- Each defect gets an orange (7) 8-neighbor halo; the defect pixels themselves stay black.\n\nKey insight: Only structural anomalies—tiny isolated patches and dangling endpoints—are marked, not bulk black regions.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"transform_in_place\",\n  \"src\": \"small black sprite s in and on edge of blue foreground regions\",\n  \"transform\": \"surround s with orange\",\n  \"src2\": \"small blue sprite s2 in and on edge of black bg regions\",\n  \"transform2\": \"s2.color -> bg (disappear)\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"16b78196\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.2989\nmechanism=0.3918\nlexical=0.0127\ncode_feature=0.2500\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, object:grid_edge, object:hole, object:sprite, operation:count, relation:direction, relation:inside, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/16b78196.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/16b78196.json\n</paths>\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
